      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pierce the aubergines all over with a fork. Place them directly over a gas flame or under a hot grill, turning occasionally, until the skin is charred and the flesh is completely soft, about 15-20 minutes. Alternatively, roast at 220C for 30-35 minutes."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Allow the aubergines to cool slightly, then peel away the charred skin and discard it. Roughly chop the flesh and drain in a colander for a few minutes to remove excess liquid."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the olive oil in a large saucepan over medium heat. Add the garlic, cumin and smoked paprika and cook, stirring, for 1 minute until fragrant."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the tinned tomatoes. Cook for 10 minutes, stirring now and then. The sauce will thicken and the oil will separate from the tomatoes."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the chopped aubergine and cook for a further 10 minutes, mashing lightly with a fork to break it down into the sauce. The texture should be rough and chunky, not smooth."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Remove from the heat. Stir in the lemon juice, most of the fresh coriander and parsley, and season with black pepper. Taste and adjust. Serve on a plate with a drizzle of olive oil and the remaining herbs, alongside warm flatbread."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "question": "The zaalouk is watery"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The aubergine releases a lot of water during cooking. Make sure to drain the chopped flesh thoroughly before adding it to the pan, and cook the final mixture uncovered so the liquid can evaporate. Increase the heat slightly if needed."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "question": "The aubergine skin will not peel cleanly"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The skin peels easily only when the aubergine is fully cooked and soft right through. Place the charred aubergine in a bowl covered with cling film for 10 minutes after cooking. The steam loosens the skin and makes peeling simple."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "question": "The flavour is bland"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Zaalouk needs generous seasoning. Make sure the garlic is properly cooked in the oil before the tomatoes go in, and do not be timid with the cumin and smoked paprika. A good amount of lemon juice at the end is equally important."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Zaalouk is a Moroccan salad eaten at room temperature or warm. It is made in every home kitchen and sold at street food stalls. Serve it with bread as a starter or a side. Leftovers keep well in the fridge for two days. The flavour deepens as it rests."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
